WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 8.12 · Marketing & Communications
Reputation, crisis communication and social media policy

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
With little reputation to fall back on, one problem can shape how people see you for years.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:27.022
Reputation is the result of everything you do, not only what you say. Crisis communication is how you respond when something goes wrong; a social media policy sets the rules before it does.

4
00:00:27.422 --> 00:00:28.022
Prepare before a crisis

5
00:00:28.022 --> 00:00:38.981
Likely scenarios: The problems most likely to hit you, and who leads the response. For example: For our bakery: a food-hygiene complaint or a concern about a trainee’s safety.

6
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:49.570
Holding statements: Short, truthful drafts ready to adapt and send quickly. For example: “We know about the issue, trainees are safe, and we will update you by Friday.”

7
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:59.048
Who to tell: Co-founders, advisers, funders, regulators, partners and any insurer, with current contacts. For example: A one-page contact list, kept both on paper and online.

8
00:00:59.048 --> 00:01:10.467
Social media policy: Rules for official accounts and guidance for anyone who mentions you. For example: No photos of trainees without consent, even on the founders’ own accounts.

9
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:11.467
Why it matters

10
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:16.400
1. Something new has little reputation to fall back on.

11
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.444
2. One problem can shape how people see you for a long time.

12
00:01:22.444 --> 00:01:28.378
3. Preparation lets you act quickly and truthfully under pressure.

13
00:01:28.778 --> 00:01:29.378
What it connects to

14
00:01:29.378 --> 00:01:32.830
1. Your safeguarding and complaints procedures.

15
00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:37.393
2. Briefing the first people who join, and volunteers.

16
00:01:37.393 --> 00:01:42.956
3. Your media relations: who speaks in a crisis.

17
00:01:43.356 --> 00:01:43.956
What to avoid

18
00:01:43.956 --> 00:01:47.407
Speculating, or hiding the truth.

19
00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:51.230
Putting your reputation before people’s safety.

20
00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:56.422
Going silent instead of giving regular updates.

21
00:01:57.022 --> 00:01:58.822
You draft your crisis essentials and social media rules.

22
00:01:58.822 --> 00:02:02.822
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:04.322 --> 00:02:07.422
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
